A test may return `Result<(), E>` instead of panicking: `Ok(())` passes, `Err` fails. This lets you use `?` inside the test, so a test that calls several fallible functions reads cleanly:

```rust
#[test]
fn parses_config() -> Result<(), String> {
    let cfg = parse_config("threads=4")?;
    if cfg.threads == 4 {
        Ok(())
    } else {
        Err(format!("expected 4 threads, got {}", cfg.threads))
    }
}
```

You cannot use `#[should_panic]` on a test that returns `Result`. To check that an operation returns an error, use `assert!(value.is_err())` or `assert!(matches!(value, Err(MyError::Missing)))`.
